the ordinary and the extraordinary exist  side by side here  rows of bodies outside butcher's  mortuary  don't even receive a second glance  people seem desensitized to the death  that's dominated their lives for months  these bodies are just lying out in the  sun waiting to be pulled in for  examination  we don't really know anything about who  these people were  but it's clear from the  very strong smell it's hard to describe  quite how bad it is  that these people have clearly been dead  for some  time  we then learn butcher that's seen some  of the war's worst atrocities has  received these bodies from mario pole  500 miles away  another place in ukraine now synonymous  with the horrors of this war  these just a tiny fraction of the  thousands of people estimated to have  died in the city  were they from the steel plant  yesterday  it's taken more than a month for these  bodies to reach this point after the  azov style steel plant fell in may  they were brought here to help relieve  other mortuaries overwhelmed  we were invited into the post-mortem  examinations  nothing prepared us for what we would  see  the two bodies being examined are  decomposing from head to toe  you can't make out their faces but they  appear to be young men  forensic experts in full protective gear  use wire to track bullet entry and exit  points  while a prosecutor and police collect  evidence  no one wants to speak on camera here  and they don't want any of the bodies  identified  but it's clear these people are under a  lot of pressure  ukraine is struggling to cope with the  number of dead  outside the mortuary we're told this  lorry is holding more bodies that have  been here for three weeks  while a local woman arrives in search of  her dead brother it feels a desperate  last resort  his answer seems to kill her hope of  finding him  out  she tells us he lay dead on his balcony  for three to four days before he was  taken away by the ukrainian army  there's such a wretched in dignity to  all this  a family member desperately seeking her  brother's body  and bodies whose family members  likely have no idea where they lay  sally lockwood sky news butcher
 

